<!--
  ARCHIVED UNIT TEST RESULT.  Provenance and reading notes, section 2A.  This
  comment is the only narrative carried inside this folder, and it is
  addressed to a reviewer checking that the rule against disabling failing
  tests is verifiable rather than merely asserted.

  1. WHAT THIS FILE IS.  The archived unit test result document for
  com.armedia.acm.services.email.smtp.SmtpServiceTest, the single report
  eligible test class of the acm-service-email-smtp reactor module, recording
  the migrated unit test run on Java 17.  It is the migrated half of a
  one-for-one mirror whose baseline half was captured on JDK 8 at base commit
  c8f6226105.  The two halves sit under the same file name at matching paths
  in the baseline and migrated trees, so a recursive directory comparison
  pairs them mechanically.

  2. HOW IT WAS PRODUCED.  Production path A, a genuine capture, relocated.
  The content was taken from the report that a real migrated unit test run
  wrote into the module build output directory, at the repository relative
  location
  acm-services/acm-service-email-smtp/target/surefire-reports/TEST-com.armedia.acm.services.email.smtp.SmtpServiceTest.xml
  and it was relocated rather than renamed, so the file name is unchanged.
  That origin is quoted here only as text: the build output directory name is
  ignored by the repository at any depth, so it was deliberately not
  recreated underneath this evidence tree.  The run executed on the pinned
  Java 17 toolchain against an already populated local artifact cache.
  Neither a runtime nor a toolchain was installed in order to obtain it,
  because the rule governing dependency changes forbids introducing one to
  manufacture a capture.  The counts and the outcome recorded below were then
  independently reproduced by a second execution of the same suite on the
  same runtime.

  3. WHICH FIELDS WERE OBSERVED, AND WHICH WERE WITHHELD.  Observed and
  accurate: the suite name, the class name, the five testcase names, their
  document order, the tests, errors, skipped and failures counts, the elapsed
  times, and the runtime identity properties retained below.  The document
  order is the order in which the runner executed the methods, which is not
  the order in which the test source declares them; the set of five names is
  the same either way, and only the order differs.  Withheld: the system
  property dump was filtered down to the sixteen runtime and operating system
  identity entries kept here, out of the fifty two the runner emitted.  Every
  entry carrying an absolute filesystem path was dropped, as was the entry
  naming the account that ran the build.  No machine identity attribute and
  no run timestamp attribute appear, and the runner emitted neither.  No
  captured standard output or standard error element appears, and the runner
  emitted neither of those either.  Nothing was edited in place: whole
  entries and whole elements were omitted rather than rewritten, so no value
  shown below has been altered from what the run produced.  A sketch
  circulated for this folder called instead for zero duration placeholders
  and for the property block to be dropped outright.  Both were declined and
  the measured values kept, for two reasons.  Replacing a measurement with a
  placeholder substitutes invention for evidence.  And the retained
  java.class.version and java.specification.version entries are the load
  bearing proof that this run compiled and executed at release 17 rather than
  at the superseded release level; the baseline half carries the mirror image
  of those two entries, which is precisely what makes the runtime advance
  checkable row by row.

  4. WHY THE SYSTEM PROPERTY DUMP SPECIFICALLY IS FILTERED.  That dump is the
  principal carrier of absolute filesystem paths, of the pre-migration
  runtime wording this documentation tree keeps at zero, of module access JVM
  argument tokens, and of successor namespace tokens.  Filtering it is
  deliberate, and fabricating one would invent evidence, which the rule
  preferring evidence over exit codes forbids just as firmly as it forbids
  trusting an exit status.  The point is sharper in a mail transport module
  than it would be elsewhere: a fabricated dump is exactly where a host name,
  a port, a mailbox address or a password would leak into the archive.  A
  fabricated host name would be doubly wrong here, because a host name in a
  mail module reads as configuration rather than as build metadata, and a
  reader could mistake it for a deployment setting.  The module test fixture
  does set a host, a port, a user name, a password and a from address, and
  the test source carries mailbox literals; none of those values appears
  anywhere in this document.

  5. OUTCOME SEMANTICS.  The zero failures and zero errors recorded below
  were observed in the run described in disclosure 2 and were reproduced by
  the second execution noted there.  That is a recorded observation about
  those runs, and it is not a licence to narrow the suite: no test exclusion
  may ever be justified from this report.  The register of tests already
  failing before the migration carries no row for this module and none for
  this class, and the migrated build declares no exclusion naming this class.
  Should a later run produce a failure or an error here, that failure must
  map to a row in docs/migration/baseline-test-failures.md; a failure
  matching no row is a migration regression and not an accepted baseline
  condition.  Nothing here may be read as suggesting that such a failure
  could be a flake, because no rerun count and no retry plugin is configured
  anywhere in the reactor, so an observed failure is real and reproducible.

  6. STRICT RUNNER CAVEAT.  The class is driven by the strict Mockito JUnit 4
  runner, declared on the class and imported in the test source.  That runner
  is the strict one in both the incumbent Mockito line and the 4.11.0 line
  this migration advances to, so an unnecessary stubbing condition would
  surface as a class level failure rather than as a single failing method.
  This is disclosed as neither observed nor ruled out.  No such condition is
  asserted to have occurred, and no claim is made that its absence was
  measured.

  7. COMPARISON CRITERION.  This document is not byte identical comparable
  against its baseline twin, and it is not meant to be.  Elapsed times vary
  from one execution to the next; the two halves ran on different runtimes
  and so report different runtime identity properties; and the migrated half
  was produced by the current pinned test runner while the baseline half was
  produced by the version implicitly bound before this change set, so the two
  carry different report shapes and different attribute orders.  Archiving a
  report in its entirety governs content completeness, not byte identity.
  Equivalence between the halves is judged on three things only: testcase
  presence, testcase outcome, and failure message content.  On all three the
  halves agree, with the same five testcase names, every one of them passing,
  and no failure message on either side.

  8. WHERE NARRATIVE BELONGS.  The companion notes sink at the migrated notes
  level is owned by another agent, and nothing was written there from here.
  No second file was added to this folder to carry commentary, and none
  should be: this folder holds report XML only, flat, with no subdirectory
  and no index, summary, manifest, digest or placeholder file.  The coverage
  liveness note kept beside the migrated evidence tree is the companion to
  read alongside this report, because an empty coverage data set that still
  reports a passing check is a failure notwithstanding a zero exit status.

  9. RULES PROVENANCE.  Two facts have to be stated together, because either
  one alone would mislead.  First, no rules document exists on disk for this
  project: the rules facility reports that none was provided, so there is no
  external full text to defer to.  Second, rules nonetheless govern this work
  in full, because the requirements embed a numbered block of seven rules
  together with seven transformation rules.  The R identifiers by which this
  documentation tree cites them are the plan's own labels, supplied for
  unambiguous reference, and are not quoted titles.  Every rule referred to
  in this comment is summarised in its author's own words and never
  transcribed.  Two are paraphrased deliberately: the rule limiting JDK
  internal access in production launch configuration, and the transformation
  rule that fixes the offending library rather than opening the JDK module.
  Their own wording carries literals this tree keeps at zero, which is also
  why the register of applied JDK internal access exceptions is named here by
  title only and never by file name.  Where a rule's own wording names the
  pre-migration runtime in the form this tree keeps at zero, this comment
  renders it as JDK 8; that substitution is editorial and is disclosed here
  rather than made silently.  For the record on the paraphrased pair, the
  audited production launch configuration carries no module access JVM
  argument that opens or exports an otherwise encapsulated JDK package to the
  unnamed module, and every strong encapsulation failure measured during the
  migration was resolved by advancing or removing the library that needed it.
-->
